--TEST--
`Core\Out::capture` swallows what its closure echoes, nests by call nesting and answers the sink's carrier rather than a `string` — spec § 12, `rule:tooling/echo-always-has-a-sink` and `rule:security/capture-answers-the-carrier`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 12: the buffer is scoped to a closure, so `ob_start`/`ob_get_clean`'s
// global stack has no equivalent and neither does implicit flushing.
var $captured = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    echo "a";
    echo "b";
});

// `rule:security/capture-answers-the-carrier`: the return is the *carrier* of the sink in force — `Cli\Text`
// outside an HTTP request — so re-emitting it is a visible `echo`, and the
// bytes are written as they came out of the sink rather than through it twice.
echo $captured, "\n";

// `capture` always swallows: nothing above reached this program's own output,
// which is why the line below is the first "a" a reader sees in order.
echo "swallowed=", $captured, "\n";

// Nesting is call nesting. The inner capture takes only what its own closure
// wrote, and the outer one takes the inner's re-emission along with the rest.
echo Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    echo "outer<";
    echo Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo "nested"; });
    echo ">";
}), "\n";

// A closure that writes nothing answers an empty carrier, not `null`.
echo "[", Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {}), "]\n";

// § 12's `{through: $filter}` transforms what was captured and takes and
// answers the same carrier. The identity is the filter that pins the plumbing
// alone — that the option is read and its closure called on the captured
// value; a filter that reads the carrier and writes a different one is
// `out-capture-through-rewrites-the-captured-text.nvst`.
echo Core\Out::capture(
    fn (): void => { echo "filtered"; },
    {through: fn (Core\Cli\Text $t): Core\Cli\Text => $t}
), "\n";

// The sink below a capture is untouched once it closes, on the throwing edge
// as much as the ordinary one.
try {
    echo Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
        echo "lost";
        var $n = "abc" as int;
        echo $n;
    });
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "threw\n";
}
echo "after\n";
--EXPECT--
ab
swallowed=ab
outer<nested>
[]
filtered
threw
after
